From: Tomonori Nakagawa <jack@supremeoverlordjabs.co>
Subject: [PATCH] ASoC: sprd: stop rejecting the 96 kHz DAC mode

sprd_codec_set_sample_rate() looks the requested rate up in a table and
then decides whether it found anything by testing the value itself:

	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(rate_tbl); i++) {
		if (rate_tbl[i].rate == rate)
			val = rate_tbl[i].sprd_rate;
	}

	if (val)
		snd_soc_component_update_bits(codec, SOC_REG(AUD_DAC_CTL), ...);
	else
		pr_err("ERR:SPRD-CODEC not support this rate %d\n", rate);

DAC_FS_MODE is a 4-bit field whose zero encoding is a real setting -
sprd-codec.h spells it DAC_FS_MODE_96k, and SPRD_CODEC_RATE_96000 is 0 to
match. So the one rate the codec advertises that maps to register value 0
is the one rate this function always refuses, with a "not support" error
for a mode the hardware does support. Every other entry in the table
happens to be non-zero, which is why nothing else trips over it.

Keep a found flag instead, and stop scanning once there is a hit.

Signed-off-by: Tomonori Nakagawa <jack@supremeoverlordjabs.co>
---
diff --git a/sound/soc/sprd/vendor/sprd-codec.c b/sound/soc/sprd/vendor/sprd-codec.c
index d32459d9a1e4..f8c97f97f012 100755
--- a/sound/soc/sprd/vendor/sprd-codec.c
+++ b/sound/soc/sprd/vendor/sprd-codec.c
@@ -1375,6 +1375,7 @@ static int sprd_codec_set_sample_rate(struct snd_soc_component *codec,
 {
 	int i = 0;
 	unsigned int val = 0;
+	bool found = false;
 	int ret;
 	struct sprd_codec_rate_tbl {
 		u32 rate; /* 8000, 11025, ... */
@@ -1399,11 +1400,14 @@ static int sprd_codec_set_sample_rate(struct snd_soc_component *codec,
 	}
 
 	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(rate_tbl); i++) {
-		if (rate_tbl[i].rate == rate)
+		if (rate_tbl[i].rate == rate) {
 			val = rate_tbl[i].sprd_rate;
+			found = true;
+			break;
+		}
 	}
 
-	if (val)
+	if (found)
 		snd_soc_component_update_bits(codec, SOC_REG(AUD_DAC_CTL), mask,
 				    val << shift);
 	else
